[{:seon.task/name "one-home-per-helper"
  :seon.task/plan "Done; what exists. One home per helper: `seon.reader/line-starts` (text and its positions) is the one line-start index, called by seon.repl and seon.source; `seon.sock/write-line!` writes a line through sock's own write loop (EINTR retried, :epipe its declared answer), and seon.lib's copies (`write-all!`, `write-line!`) are gone; seon.intr's uncalled poll installer (install-poll*) and its alias of `seon.lib/SIGUSR1` are gone; `seon.contract/uncontracted` is the one 'function var with no :malli/schema' rule (seon.stage no longer re-derives it). Each was a pure move or delete."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reader-test/line-starts-are-each-lines-offset seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-from-a-threaded-process-never-hangs"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by root's bin/test --all on a clean main (888a728e2): shard 3 hung 61 minutes. Its runner runs tests concurrently, on threads, and the last started were seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-running-thread-is-refused-and-says-how-many-run and seon.lib-test/a-forked-child-runs-go-blocks-and-makes-a-store. A forked child of the runner (pid 4782) sat at 0% CPU, its main thread in S_condition_wait -> _pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_wait -> __psynch_mutexwait: it inherited a Chez mutex locked by a thread that does not exist in the child. The cause is measured (design agent-loop section 8, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads): ```seon.lib/fork!``` refuses only when another Chez thread is ACTIVE, but a thread is inactive while it still holds a channel's mutex on its way into and out of S_condition_wait (reference-code/chez/c/thread.c:709-720). Measured in an agent with 8 go blocks busy: 199 of 200 forks refused and 50 of 50 forced through deadlocked. So the check misses exactly the threads a concurrent runner starts; the static fork server (part 1 of the agent loop) is about memory and does not touch this class. What removes it: a process that forks is one OS thread when it forks. Agents now are (Jolt's main carrier, jolt.host/main-carrier-run!: 50 of 50 forks mid-traffic exit 0). The test runner is not: a test that forks runs, in a runner with other threads, beside them. Plan: (1) ```seon.lib/fork!``` refuses when the process has any other OS thread (```seon.lib/nthreads```), with the count and that reason, except the one call that may (root forking the fork server at boot, its threads parked and quiet: say so where it is called); (2) the runner runs a test that forks in a process of its own that is one thread (a fork-server-style child of the shard, or the shard under jolt.host/main-carrier-run!), never beside concurrent tests; (3) the two lib-test tests above run there. Deletes nothing; replaces the active-thread count as the fork's guard."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-running-thread-is-refused-and-says-how-many-run seon.lib-test/a-forked-child-runs-go-blocks-and-makes-a-store}} {:seon.task/name "a-fork-carries-no-shutdown-hook"
  :seon.task/plan "Found in the request-path audit (root, 2026-10-27: every ask answered once, every wait ends on its peer's exit or its answer): a forked child ran its parent's shutdown hooks (Jolt keeps them in one process-wide list, and a fork copied it). seon.main/root-serve's hook is root's whole stop (c/stop! sys :signal), so the fork server, every agent and every test child that left by Jolt's exit (System/exit, an uncaught throw, SIGTERM/INT/HUP) ran root's stop on its copy of root's state, against the shared store and the descriptors it inherited: the test child's silent exit 1 with hooks run, and a candidate for agents' replies that root never received (first-request's core of a1). Fixed in Jolt, the owner (its jolt-after-fork-child!, which lib/fork!'s child already runs, drops the parent's hooks: jolt aef45f45, then 260a1667, which also runs none of the parent's fibers in the child). Proof here: the fork case child-hooks. Also: root's api and agent procs name each unanswered ask in their pings (rid -> kind; the stall task records the api's as :api :open), so a stuck agent's owed answers are named next time. Open, Jolt's: a fork taken after the parent used a timer can deadlock the child on a mutex (S_mutex_acquire, its only thread): the scratch paper hang-2026-10-27/fork-child-timeout. (git history)"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-forked-child-runs-none-of-its-parents-shutdown-hooks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-child-ends-with-its-starter"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-28: stalls and orphans, at the source, one mechanism (raw evidence not kept; summarized here) spawn-inventory.md, incident-causes.md). ./seon problems trees (59471, 68845, 70911) ran 12 h at 0% CPU with ppid 1: Claude sessions ran `timeout N bin/check problems`, timeout TERMed its process group (bin/check, bin/held and held's starter watch), and ./seon problems, in a group of its own, lived on, its fork server and agents waiting on it. Nothing in a ./seon process watched what started it, and a class C process (a runtime mutex held) runs no Scheme, so no EOF or SIGIO handler can end it. Correct: one C thread per process (native/probe.c e13_end_with) waits on kqueue NOTE_EXIT for the pid it watches and SIGKILLs the process group when the process leads one, else the process; seon.main/-main watches its parent (none when launchd is the parent), and seon.lib/fork! arms it again in each child, which watches the same pid (agents watch root, not the fork server, so a standby's promotion keeps them). Replaces nothing yet: bin/held's starter fifo stays, since it ends held's command when held's starter dies and held does not. The THREADS check counts the C thread beside each forking process's one Chez thread (the collector never sees it). docs/failure-points.md states it. bin/stalls is deleted after this and seon.sock/a-frame-is-written-whole merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-ends-with-what-it-watches-and-so-does-what-it-forks}}
 {:seon.task/name "scale-charges-what-the-machine-lost"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-29 (a C experiment on this machine): macOS charges a forked child's phys_footprint for every inherited anonymous page it touches, read or written (four children reading a parent's 200 MB: 201 MB each; the machine's free memory moved ~50 MB, noise; writing: ~1 GB). Chez's heap segments are anonymous private memory (reference-code/chez/c/segment.c S_getmem), so an agent reading its inherited static heap is charged pages that cost nothing: the start-up footprint traced on 2026-10-29 (4,040-4,270 pages, 85% first reads) is mostly charge, not RAM, and `bin/check scale`'s sum of agent footprints (test/seon/check.clj, :agent-real-mb-sum) overstates ten agents (2.4 GB measured). Correct: scale charges what the machine lost: free plus inactive pages from host_statistics64 (one probe call beside seon.lib/mp-probe in native/probe.c) before root's agents start and at their peak, against :seon.config.machine/memory-mb; the per-process footprint stays the gauge of one process (an agent's own allowance, jetsam). The gauge test gains a reading child (charged, not copied). Proofs: the gauge test's read case; scale's verdict from the machine's delta."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/the-memory-gauge-reads-what-macos-charges-across-a-fork}}]
